      "id": 1,
      "text": "Stockton was renamed in 1851 when a post office opened, honoring U.S. Senator Robert Field Stockton.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/stockton_new_jersey",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before its 1851 renaming, Stockton was a ferry hub called Reading Ferry, linking settlers across the Delaware River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/stockton_new_jersey",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Belvidere Delaware Railroad arrived in 1852, turning Stockton into a rail‑connected commercial center.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/stockton_new_jersey",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Prallsville Mills Complex, listed in 1973, preserves 19th‑century mills and a historic grist mill.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/stockton_new_jersey",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Stockton Inn, founded in 1710, inspired Rodgers and Hart’s 1936 song “There’s a Small Hotel.”.",
